concepts.content:
All the different constraints described previously can be written as an overdetermined system of linear equations. Each equation has the form:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} = b_i
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{Y}$ is a vectorized version of the image $Y$. For example, if a pixel lies inside a planar face, it will generate three equations based on the planarity constraint, such as $\mathbf{a}_i = [0, \dots, 0, -1, 2, -1, 0, \dots, 0]$ and $b_i=0$.

The system can be solved by minimizing the cost function $J = \sum_i w_i (\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} - b_i)^2$, where $w_i$ are optional weights for different constraints. In matrix form, this is $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{Y} = \mathbf{b}$. Because the system is overdetermined, the solution is computed using the pseudoinverse:

$$\begin{aligned}
\bf Y = (\mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{A})^{-1} \mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}$$

This can be solved efficiently because the matrix $\mathbf{A}$ is very sparse.

